---- 3.3.1: what must never be stored ----
authorized            true (want true)
cvv retained anywhere false (by construction — CardRecord has no field for it)

also forbidden after authorization: full magnetic-stripe/chip track
data, and the PIN or PIN block. encryption does not make storing
them acceptable — the rule is about existence, not readability

---- 3.4.1: masking for display ----
full pan (never logged)  4111111111111111
what a receipt may show  411111******1111
luhn valid               true (want true)
luhn rejects a typo      false (want false)

---- 3.5.1.1: why an unkeyed hash is not 'unreadable' ----
stolen hash   9bbef19476623ca56c17da75fd57734dbf82530686043a6e491c6d71befe8f6e

the attacker also has what compliant masking already showed them:
the first six and last four digits. for an 8-digit BIN that leaves
four unknown digits — 10 000 candidates, which is what we search
here so this example finishes. a 6-digit BIN leaves six unknown, so
1 000 000 candidates, and that is still seconds of work

recovered pan 4111111111111111
matches       true (want true)

that is why 3.5.1.1 requires a KEYED hash. without the key the same
search is impossible: the attacker cannot compute a candidate's
index at all

keyed index   740f0fc6debb982474bf831f16871ec726550b56a5fb3de3efd878ff551771d2
same search   "" (want empty — no candidate matches)

---- 3.5.1 + 3.6: storing the card ----
token         tok_9f2a41c7
generation    2026-Q3
masked        411111******1111
lookup index  740f0fc6debb982474bf831f16871ec7…
ciphertext    5d3d27f693b69ff58880924216717022…  (32 bytes)

everything above is safe in a database, a backup and a log — the
token and masked pan are what the rest of the business uses, and
nothing here reveals the card without the KEK

---- dedupe without decrypting ----
same card, different token  true (want true)
different card              false (want false)
ciphertexts still differ    true (want true)

identical cards share an index but never a ciphertext, because the
nonce differs — equal ciphertexts would leak the match to anyone
who could read the table

---- tamper resistance ----
authorized read       true (want true)
generation relabelled "" (want empty)
moved to new token    "" (want empty)
wrong kek             "" (want empty)

---- 3.6.1.4 / 3.7.4: key rotation ----
old generation reads  true (want true)
new generation reads  true (want true)
ciphertexts differ    true (want true)
index unchanged       true (want true)

the lookup index survives rotation because it is keyed from the KEK
rather than the DEK — rotating data keys does not invalidate every
search index, which is what makes rotation something you will
actually do rather than postpone forever

---- Requirement 10: an audit log you cannot quietly edit ----
head mac  5ae06f8fe0c138c782346cda612b7279…
after deleting the 'reveal' line, mac matches: false (want false)
after editing the case number, mac matches:    false (want false)

---- what this example does not do ----
scope reduction   the real win is not storing PANs at all: let the
                  processor tokenize, and most of Requirement 3
                  stops applying to your systems
key custody       the KEK here is a constant in a source file, which
                  fails 3.6 outright. it belongs in an HSM or KMS,
                  under split knowledge and dual control
access control    who may call revealPan, and how that is enforced
transit           Requirement 4 needs TLS — vyto/crypto/openssl has it
everything else   eleven other requirements, and an assessor
